\f0\b\fs48 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Autoregulation, Drift Management, and Periodization Policies for Helm: Deterministic Decision Engines for Hypertrophy Training\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 Executive Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 Autoregulation in resistance training balances structural adaptation against dynamic physiological fatigue. While traditional fixed linear periodization assumes ideal recovery, human performance operates under stochastic stress from training loads, sleep disruption, psychological strain, and environmental factors. Helm employs an offline-first, deterministic policy engine designed to govern daily session adjustments, microcycle schedule deviations (drift), and macrocycle deload sequences.\
The core architecture treats morning Autonomic Recovery Condition (ARC) scores\'97derived primarily from sleep-window standard deviation of NN intervals (SDNN)\'97as a noisy, within-person prior rather than an absolute indicator of muscular force capacity. In resistance training for hypertrophy, autonomic markers such as heart rate variability (HRV) correlate weakly with acute single-joint force production and localized muscle damage. Consequently, the policy engine uses ARC to modulate systemic parameters\'97such as RPE caps, volume set trimming, and equipment selection\'97while relying on hard-set accounting to safeguard weekly Minimum Effective Volume (MEV) floors and Maximum Recoverable Volume (MRV) ceilings.\
The system architecture flows deterministically from morning input evaluation to final clamped session execution. Morning ARC metrics and subjective scores pass into the Readiness Band Classifier to establish baseline daily constraints. Concurrently, calendar drift logic checks the microcycle state to determine if sessions are delayed or out of sequence. Prescribed training loads are then passed through an Exponentially Weighted Moving Average (EWMA) Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio (ACWR) guard to prevent unmanaged volume spikes. Finally, the resulting clamped session target is passed to an offline set ledger, while the Large Language Model (LLM) Coach narrative layer interprets the engine's choices for the user without overriding numeric clamps.\
Calendar disruptions are handled by a deterministic drift matrix. Delayed or out-of-order sessions trigger automatic calendar shifts, volume redistributions, or week index freezes without relying on unstructured volume catch-up spikes. EWMA ACWR calculations act as structural guards to prevent acute volume overspikes. Macrocycle deloads transition from static set cuts to dynamic recovery cycles that preserve motor pattern retention while shedding systemic fatigue. The operational model provides clear boundaries between deterministic numeric engine rules and LLM-driven coach explanations, ensuring testability, safety, and consistent adherence to progressive overload principles.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 Readiness Signal Limits and Band-to-Action Mapping\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 Limits of Autonomic Signals in Hypertrophy Programming\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 Heart rate variability measures autonomic nervous system (ANS) balance, specifically cardiac parasympathetic modulation. While HRV-guided protocols demonstrate utility in endurance sports by optimizing maximal oxygen uptake ($\\text\{VO\}_2\\text\{max\}$) and managing cardiorespiratory strain, their direct translation to resistance training for muscle hypertrophy is limited.\

