\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 Landmark Resets Post-Deload\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 Upon successful completion of a scheduled or reactive deload week, the periodization engine resets fatigue tracking variables and progression anchors prior to initiating the subsequent mesocycle block:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Volume Landmark Reset:
\f1\b0  Muscle group volume targets reset to baseline $\\text\{MEV\}_\{\\text\{baseline\}\}$. The cumulative hard-set volume accumulator clears to zero.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Load Progression Re-anchor:
\f1\b0  Initial working loads for the new mesocycle are set to $92.5\\% - 95.0\\%$ of the pre-deload top working weights. This re-establishes a progressive overload ramp while accounting for structural fatigue clearance.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Autonomic Baseline Recalibration:
\f1\b0  The 30-day rolling baseline mean ($\\mu_\{\\text\{SDNN\}, 30d\}$) and standard deviation ($\\sigma_\{\\text\{SDNN\}, 30d\}$) are recalculated, excluding deload-week data points to prevent skewing future readiness metrics.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 Deload Modulation by Nutritional Phase\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Energy Surplus (Mass Gain Phase):
\f1\b0  Deload set reductions are set at $50\\%$ of peak volume. High energy availability supports rapid systemic recovery, allowing full neural supercompensation within 7 days.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Energy Deficit (Fat Loss Cut Phase):
\f1\b0  Deload set reductions are capped at $60\\%$ of peak volume (preserving slightly higher volume to maintain muscle retention signaling), but working loads are reduced by an additional $-5\\%$ to account for impaired recovery capacity in an energy deficit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio (ACWR) and Spike Guards\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 To prevent rapid volume expansions from increasing injury risk, the engine tracks workload dynamics using an Exponentially Weighted Moving Average (EWMA) ACWR model. The EWMA model places greater weight on recent training exposures, providing higher sensitivity to acute workload spikes than unweighted rolling averages.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 Mathematical Specification of EWMA ACWR\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 Daily Session Load ($L_t$) is calculated as the sum of hard sets weighted by set intensity (RPE):\
$$L_t = \\sum_\{s=1\}^\{\\text\{Sets\}\} \\left( 1.0 + 0.1 \\cdot (\\text\{RPE\}_s - 7.0) \\right)$$\
The acute workload decay constant ($\\lambda_a$) and chronic workload decay constant ($\\lambda_c$) are defined based on traditional time windows ($N_a = 7 \\text\{ days\}$, $N_c = 28 \\text\{ days\}$):\
$$\\lambda_a = \\frac\{2\}\{N_a + 1\} = \\frac\{2\}\{7 + 1\} = 0.25$$\
$$\\lambda_a = \\frac\{2\}\{N_a + 1\} = \\frac\{2\}\{7 + 1\} = 0.25$$\
The acute exponentially weighted workload ($w_\{\\text\{acute\}, t\}$) and chronic exponentially weighted workload ($w_\{\\text\{chronic\}, t\}$) are updated daily:\
$$w_\{\\text\{acute\}, t\} = L_t \\cdot \\lambda_a + (1 - \\lambda_a) \\cdot w_\{\\text\{acute\}, t-1\}$$\
$$w_\{\\text\{acute\}, t\} = L_t \\cdot \\lambda_a + (1 - \\lambda_a) \\cdot w_\{\\text\{acute\}, t-1\}$$\
The resulting ratio defines the acute-to-chronic load equilibrium:\
$$\\text\{ACWR\}_t = \\frac\{w_\{\\text\{acute\}, t\}\}\{w_\{\\text\{chronic\}, t\}\}$$\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 Deterministic ACWR Spike Guard Policy\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The engine continuously evaluates $\\text\{ACWR\}_t$ to place athletes into distinct operational workload zones:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Under-training / Detraining Zone ($\\text\{ACWR\} < 0.80$):
\f1\b0  The engine permits weekly volume ramp-ups up to $+15\\%$ toward target MEV to re-establish baseline training volume.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Optimal Adaptation Zone ($0.80 \\le \\text\{ACWR\} \\le 1.30$):
\f1\b0  Standard microcycle load progression is maintained, allowing normal volume increments of $+1$ to $+2$ sets per muscle group.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Warning / High Load Zone ($1.31 \\le \\text\{ACWR\} \\le 1.50$):
\f1\b0  Weekly volume growth is capped at $+5\\%$ maximum over the prior week, and RPE is clamped at $\\le 8.5$. Volume catch-up is restricted to a maximum of $+1$ set total backfill.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Spike Danger Zone ($\\text\{ACWR\} > 1.50$):
\f1\b0  The engine enforces a hard volume clamp, capping today's session load to $\\le w_\{\\text\{chronic\}, t\}$ and dropping non-essential accessory slots. Volume catch-up is strictly prohibited, and missed sets are discarded.\

