\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 Coach Behaviour Contract\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The Helm architecture separates deterministic computational decision engines from the conversational LLM Coach layer. The deterministic engine calculates numeric values, set counts, exercise selections, and safety clamps offline before the user opens the application. The LLM Coach acts as an interpretive interface, explaining decision outputs without altering structural bounds.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 Division of Responsibilities\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The offline deterministic engine serves as the system-of-record. It calculates ARC $z$-scores, subjective composite indices, and ACWR values, while executing set trimming, slot dropping, equipment swaps, and RPE clamping. It enforces structural safety clamps and calendar drift transformations, outputting a definitive, immutable session payload.\
The online LLM Coach operates purely as a narrative and explanatory layer. It translates engine outputs into concise narrative explanations, such as detailing why an RPE cap was applied. The coach answers athlete questions regarding movement feel and execution, and offers pre-approved equipment alternatives within clamped boundaries. If an athlete reports qualitative pain or distress in their notes, the coach can suggest rest even if the engine scheduled a workout.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

