\f0\b \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 Coach Narrative Guidelines and Boundaries\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Deterministic Precedence:
\f1\b0  The Coach must not override numerical prescriptions generated by the engine. It cannot add sets, raise RPE caps, or ignore ACWR volume limits.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Rest Override Authority:
\f1\b0  The Coach may suggest a rest day against a scheduled workout only when the athlete's qualitative notes report acute joint pain ($>4/10$), symptoms of systemic illness, or severe mental fatigue. If the user accepts, the session status updates to 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 COACH_RECOMMENDED_REST
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  and triggers the 1-day late calendar shift.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No Medical Diagnostics:
\f1\b0  The Coach operates under a strict non-diagnostic boundary. It must frame structural discomfort strictly around fatigue management and equipment substitution, referring athletes to qualified medical professionals when red-flag pain signals occur.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 Migration Architecture and Acceptance Test Suites\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 Upgrading legacy Helm gating logic to this deterministic engine resolves several historical failure modes. The legacy binary HRV pass/fail thresholds are replaced by continuous $z$-score evaluation across four operational bands, scaling volume multipliers ($M_V$) and RPE caps incrementally.\
The static $\\sim 50\\%$ deload logic, which previously under-dosed advanced lifters below MEV, is replaced by dynamic deload scaling that preserves set floors at $\\text\{MEV\}_\{\\text\{muscle\}\}$ while capping RPE at $5.0-6.0$. Unmonitored catch-up volume spikes are eliminated through the integration of EWMA ACWR guards ($\\lambda_a = 0.25, \\lambda_c = 0.069$) that enforce hard clamps when $\\text\{ACWR\} > 1.50$.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 Acceptance Test Suite\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The engine's deterministic behavior is verified using automated test fixtures:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Test Case 1 (Depleted Readiness Set Cap):
\f1\b0  Given an input fixture with 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 arc_z_score = -1.82
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 subjective_soreness = 1.5/5.0
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , and 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 prescribed_sets = 16
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , the engine evaluates readiness band to Depleted ($M_V = 0.50$). The output sets must equal exactly 8, and 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 RPE_max
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  must be clamped to $\\le 7.0$. Total output sets must not exceed baseline planned sets under any branch condition.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Test Case 2 (EWMA ACWR Spike Guard):
\f1\b0  Given an acute load 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 w_acute = 145.0
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  and chronic load 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 w_chronic = 90.0
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  ($\\text\{ACWR\} = 1.61$), where missed session recovery is attempted via a $+6$ set backfill, the engine detects $\\text\{ACWR\} > 1.50$. The engine rejects volume backfill, clamping today's load to $\\le w_\{\\text\{chronic\}\}$ ($90.0$) and marking uncompleted backfill sets as 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 DISCARDED_SPIKE_GUARD
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 .\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Test Case 3 (5-Day Calendar Drift Resolution):
\f1\b0  Given Session 14 (Pull Day) logged $132\\text\{h\}$ late (5.5 days) during Week 3, the engine evaluates the drift matrix condition 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 5+ Days Late
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 . The engine skips all intermediate missed sessions, freezes the week index at Week 3, marks missed targets as 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 SKIPPED_EXPIRED
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , and re-anchors the calendar sequence to the current date.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Test Case 4 (Scheduled Deload Volume Floor):
\f1\b0  Given a transition from Week 5 (Peak Week, 20 sets/quads) to Week 6 (Scheduled Deload) where Quad $\\text\{MEV\} = 8$ sets, the engine computes deload volume ($0.50 \\cdot 20 = 10$ sets). The prescribed deload sets equal 10 (satisfying $\\ge \\text\{MEV\}$), and set RPE targets are clamped to $\\le 6.0$.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Test Case 5 (Signal Conflict Resolution):
\f1\b0  Given 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 arc_z_score = -1.60
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Depleted), 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 subjective_soreness = 4.5/5.0
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (High), and 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 sleep_quality = 4.0/5.0
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (High), the engine computes composite index $\\text\{RI\} = 0.6 \\cdot ((-1.60 + 3)/6) + 0.4 \\cdot (0.85) = 0.48$. The composite index maps to Normal/Baseline ($0.45 \\le \\text\{RI\} \\le 0.75$), executing at standard baseline volume ($M_V = 1.00$) while clamping 
\fs30 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 RPE_max
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  to $8.5$ as an autonomic safeguard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 Evidence Appendix and Open Decision Defaults\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 Sports Science Literature Synthesis\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 Extensive literature demonstrates that HRV-guided endurance protocols yield superior improvements in cardiorespiratory fitness compared to static periodization. In contrast, studies examining HRV-guided resistance training show comparable strength and hypertrophic adaptations between HRV-guided and pre-determined groups. Autonomic metrics reflect systemic recovery capacity rather than localized muscular repair or neuromuscular force output.\
Additionally, research on workload monitoring confirms that Exponentially Weighted Moving Averages (EWMA) offer superior sensitivity for detecting acute workload spikes compared to rolling average models. EWMA models account for the temporal decay of fitness and fatigue over time, making them better suited for dynamic volume adjustments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

